appendix=r'''<section id="appendix-E">
<h2>Appendix E. Exact Gaussian and sinusoidal reference fields</h2>
<p>We construct the two non-soliton benchmarks by choosing a transverse parameter in a heat-equation representation of the DLW system. The construction uses a Laplace transformation of the associated Boiti–Leon–Pempinelli system, as studied in [16]. The parameter choice below produces the initial profiles of Section 5.1 and gives their subsequent continuous evolution.</p>
<p><strong>Linear evolutions.</strong> Write \(X=x-4t\), \(s(y)=A\sin[\pi(y+2)/4]/2\), and denote the prescribed longitudinal profile by \(F_0\). Let \(C\) be its mean and choose \(J_0(X)=CX+J_{\rm per}(X)\), with \(J_0'=F_0\) and zero-mean periodic \(J_{\rm per}\). Here \(C=0\) for the cosine and \(C=\sqrt\pi/32\) for the periodic Gaussian. Treating \(s\) as an independent parameter, solve</p>
<div class="equation">\[\begin{aligned}F_t&=-F_{XX},&F(X,0)&=F_0(X),\\ H_t&=H_{XX},&H(X,s,0)&=-s e^{-sJ_0(X)}.\end{aligned}\tag{E1}\]</div>
<p>The chosen profiles give explicit backward-heat evolutions,</p>
<div class="equation">\[\begin{aligned}F_{\rm S}(X,t)&=e^{(\pi/12)^2t}\cos(\pi X/12),\\ F_{\rm G}(X,t)&=\frac{3}{\sqrt{9-t}}\sum_{j\in\mathbb Z}\exp\!\left[-\frac{(X+192j)^2}{4(9-t)}\right],\quad t<9.\end{aligned}\tag{E2}\]</div>
<p>The forward-heat solution \(H\) is obtained from the Fourier series of \(e^{-sJ_{\rm per}}\), retaining the factor \(e^{-sCX}\). Define \(\psi\) by</p>
<div class="equation">\[\psi_t=FH_X-F_XH,\qquad\psi(X,s,0)=e^{-sJ_0(X)}.\tag{E3}\]</div>
<p>Equation (E1) gives \(\partial_t(FH)=\partial_X(FH_X-F_XH)\). Since \(\psi_X=FH\) at \(t=0\), this identity holds throughout the evolution and fixes the spatial derivative needed in the reconstruction.</p>
<p><strong>Physical fields and initial matching.</strong> Set \(D=\psi H_s-H\psi_s\). The transformed fields are</p>
<div class="equation">\[\begin{aligned}u(x,y,t)&=-2\left(\frac{D_X}{D}-\frac{\psi_X}{\psi}\right),\\ v(x,y,t)&=4+4s_y\frac{FD}{\psi^2}-s_y u_s,\qquad X=x-4t,\quad s=s(y).\end{aligned}\tag{E4}\]</div>
<p>In the second line, \(u_s\) differentiates the expression in the first line before substituting \(s=s(y)\). Substitution using (E1)–(E3) gives both continuous DLW equations (1), with \(a=2\) and \(\lambda=-2\). The final expressions divide only by \(\psi\) and \(D\), so zeros of the cosine profile or of \(s_y\) leave the reconstructed fields regular.</p>
<p>Initially, \(H=-s\psi\) and \(D=-\psi^2\). Consequently,</p>
<div class="equation">\[u_0=2sF_0,\qquad v_0=4-6s_yF_0=4-3u_{0y}.\tag{E5}\]</div>
<p>This calculation explains the joint choice of the two initial fields. The coefficient three is generated by the transformed family. It does not impose a constraint on the subsequent evolution: the DLW equations give \(\partial_t(v-4+3u_y)|_{t=0}=8u_{0xxy}\), which is generally nonzero. At \(y=\pm2\), \(s=0\) gives \(u=0\), while \(s_y=0\) at \(y=0\) gives \(v=4\). The quasi-periodic factors in \(H\) and \(\psi\) cancel in (E4), yielding periodic physical fields.</p>
<p><strong>Regularity and reference evaluation.</strong> For the amplitudes \(A=0.05\) and \(A=0.03\), we have \(|s|\le0.025\). Fourier coefficient bounds for (E1), combined with Cauchy estimates on the parameter disk \(|s|\le0.05\), control \(H,\psi\) and their parameter derivatives. Comparing them with \(\psi_0=e^{-sJ_0}\) and \(D_0=-e^{-2sJ_0}\) gives the uniform bounds</p>
<div class="equation">\[\psi>0.6,\qquad |D|>0.4,\qquad |x|\le96,\quad |y|\le2,\quad 0\le t\le0.01.\tag{E6}\]</div>
<p>For example, the coefficient estimates \(\|F\|_{\mathcal A_2}<2\), \(\|J_{\rm per}\|_{\mathcal A_2}<11\), and \(0\le C<0.056\), where \(\|q\|_{\mathcal A_2}=\sum_n(1+|\xi_n|)^2|q_n|\), give \(|J_0|<16.432\) on \(|X|\le97\). Integrating (E3) bounds the change in \(\psi\); the forward-heat bounds control the change in \(H\). Cauchy estimates for their \(s\) derivatives then bound the change in \(D\), giving (E6). These lower bounds justify the smooth reconstruction on the full test region.</p>
<p>We evaluate (E4) through Fourier series and analytic parameter derivatives. Each heat mode has an explicit time multiplier, so the reference evaluation introduces no time-stepping error. The stored references use a degree-32 exponential expansion and 96 positive Gaussian Fourier modes; the sinusoidal input has one mode. Independent interval evaluation, including analytical tails and floating-point conversion, bounds the error in every stored initial field and time derivative and every stored final field at \(T=0.01\) by \(10^{-13}\). This bound applies at the native sample coordinates used in the error tables.</p>
</section>'''
